---
id: styles-01
courseId: frontend-architecture
title: "Styles that ==leak=="
concepts: [css-global-scope, style-isolation-options, shadow-dom-isolation]
# Sources for every claim about tools (checked 2026-10-06; quoted text was read on the linked page that day).
# Predict and fillBlanks feedback is plain text, so it names the source and the URL is kept here.
# - MDN, Type selectors (e1, p1): "The CSS type selector matches elements by node name. In other words, it selects all
#   elements of the given type within a document.":
#   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/Type_selectors
# - Rsbuild, CSS usage (e1, f3, p6): "by default, Rsbuild will extract CSS into a separate .css file and output it to the
#   dist directory"; for ?inline: "Get the compiled text content of the CSS file ... The content will be inlined into the
#   final JavaScript bundle. No separate CSS file will be generated.":
#   https://rsbuild.rs/guide/styling/css-usage
# - Module Federation, Manifest: mf-manifest.json "describes which modules the producer exposes, where the remote entry
#   is, which assets those modules need" (e1):
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html
# - Rsbuild, CSS Modules (f1, e2, p3): *.module.css files are CSS Modules by default; "CSS Modules class names are
#   automatically appended with a hash value to prevent class name conflicts"; production names look like .red-hiQIE4;
#   default import used as className={styles.red}:
#   https://rsbuild.rs/guide/styling/css-modules
# - CSS Modules specification repository (e2, p3): "A CSS Module is a CSS file where all class names and animation names
#   are scoped locally by default." (type selectors are not named; the recording shows they stay global):
#   https://github.com/css-modules/css-modules
# - MDN, Using shadow DOM (f2, p4, pf1): "The page CSS does not affect nodes inside the shadow DOM"; "none of the code
#   inside a shadow DOM can affect anything outside it"; style elements in a template "are applied only within the
#   shadow DOM tree":
#   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components/Using_shadow_DOM
# - MDN, Element.attachShadow() (f2): "attaches a shadow DOM tree to the specified element and returns a reference to its
#   ShadowRoot"; mode open or closed:
#   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/attachShadow
# - react.dev, createPortal (f2): "createPortal lets you render some children into a different part of the DOM":
#   https://react.dev/reference/react-dom/createPortal
# - MDN, CSS scoping (pf1): "Globally scoped styles apply to all the elements in the node tree that match the selector
#   ... but not to the shadow trees"; "Selectors and their associated style definitions don't bleed between scopes.":
#   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Scoping
# - MDN, Inheritance (p5, rc1): an inherited property such as color takes the parent's computed value; a non-inherited
#   property such as border takes its initial value:
#   https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascade/Inheritance
# - W3C, CSS Scoping Module Level 1, section 3.3.2 Inheritance (p5, rc1): "The top-level elements of a shadow tree
#   inherit from their host element." MDN states no such sentence on the pages above, so the specification is cited.
#   https://www.w3.org/TR/css-scoping-1/
# Recordings. Every runtime value in this lesson was recorded from scratch copies of examples/host, remote-a and
# remote-b (this lesson may not edit examples/**). Changes in the copies, nothing else:
# - host: a <button data-testid="host-pay">Pay</button> after the version line; one variant also imports host.css with
#   main { color: rgb(0, 0, 255); } and button { border: 3px solid rgb(0, 128, 0); }.
# - remote_b: Widget.tsx variants that import widget.css (button rule, or the same rule prefixed with
#   [data-remote='remote_b']), widget.module.css (.button rule, used as className={styles.button}; or a button rule),
#   or render heading, version line and button into a shadow root with attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }) and createPortal,
#   importing widget.css either normally or with ?inline into a <style> inside the root.
# - every rsbuild.config.ts: output.distPath.root read from an environment variable, so each variant has its own dist.
# Built with the example's pinned Rsbuild 2.2.12 and @module-federation/rsbuild-plugin 2.9.2 (the DTS plugin logs
# TYPE-001 for the .module.css and ?inline variants because they have no type declarations; the bundles are built).
# Served on ports 3000, 3001 and 3002 by a static server like examples/recorder/src/record.ts (plus text/css), and
# loaded in headless Chromium 141.0.7390.37 through Playwright 1.56.1 (the copy in apps/web), a fresh browser context per
# scenario. Values are getComputedStyle results after the network was idle. The whole recording was run twice; all
# computed styles, link tags and class names were identical in both runs. Only the order of requests between different
# servers changed, which no step claims.
# Labelled as our reading, not a documented guarantee: why the manifest's css asset ends up as a <link> in
# document.head (we record that it did).
---

{% hook id="h1" estSeconds=40 character="bug" concepts=["css-global-scope"] %}
Tuesday, 11:00. Team B wants red buttons in their widget. They add one rule to remote_b's stylesheet and deploy remote_b. Only remote_b. At 11:04 finance calls: the host's Pay button is red too, and customers think something is wrong. The Bug did nothing. It only watched.
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
A stylesheet ships with remote_b. Which buttons on the page can its rules reach?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /hook %}

{% predict id="p1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/global-leak.ts" language="ts" concepts=["css-global-scope"] %}
{% option output="remote_b" misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
The file was built and deployed by remote_b, but the rule has no idea. Once loaded, it matched every button in the document.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Pay (host), remote_b" misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
remote_a's button is a button too. A type selector does not care which team rendered the element.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Pay (host), remote_a, remote_b" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. One rule from remote_b turned all three buttons red, the host's and the other remote's too.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
Wait, remote_a didn't even change. Its button turned red anyway?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/where-css-went.txt" concepts=["css-global-scope"] %}
Rsbuild [extracts CSS into a separate .css file](https://rsbuild.rs/guide/styling/css-usage). The remote's [manifest](https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html) lists the assets each exposed module needs, and in our recording that file arrived as a `<link>` in the host's `document.head`. From there it is the page's stylesheet. A [type selector](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/Type_selectors) "selects all elements of the given type within a document". The document is the whole page, not remote_b's corner of it.

{% annotation line=3 %}
The exposed Widget needs this CSS file.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=6 %}
Lands in the host's head, page-wide.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% predict id="p2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/prefix-and-host-rule.ts" language="ts" concepts=["style-isolation-options", "css-global-scope"] %}
{% option output="red, 2px black border" misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
The prefix keeps remote_b's rule inside remote_b. Nothing keeps the host's rule out. Its button rule matched remote_b's button and gave it the green border.
{% /option %}
{% option output="grey, 3px green border" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
The prefixed rule still matches remote_b's button, so the background stays red. Only the host's border rule came in on top of it.
{% /option %}
{% option output="red, 3px green border" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. The prefix stopped the leak out: Pay stayed grey. But the host's global rule leaked in and gave remote_b a green border.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So a prefix is a fence with only one side?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% fillBlanks id="f1" estSeconds=60 template="// remote_b/src/Widget.tsx with CSS Modules\nimport styles from './widget.___ext___';\n\n<button className={styles.___key___}>clicked {count}</button>" language="ts" concepts=["style-isolation-options"] %}
{% blank id="ext" accepted=["module.css"] misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
Rsbuild treats files named *.module.css as CSS Modules. A plain widget.css stays a global stylesheet, as in the first run.
{% /blank %}
{% blank id="key" accepted=["button"] misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
The import is an object of the file's class names. Our file declares .button, so styles.button holds its hashed name. Source: Rsbuild, CSS Modules.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p3" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/module-type-selector.ts" language="ts" concepts=["style-isolation-options", "css-global-scope"] %}
{% option output="remote_b" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
CSS Modules rename class names and animation names. A bare button selector has no class to rename, so it went out as it was.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Pay (host), remote_a, remote_b" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. The .module.css name did not help: the type selector stayed global and turned every button red again.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/options-so-far.txt" concepts=["style-isolation-options"] %}
Two options so far, both recorded. A **prefix** works if every team keeps the rule. [CSS Modules](https://rsbuild.rs/guide/styling/css-modules) append a hash to class names: our `.button` became `button-TUj2Xi`. The [CSS Modules definition](https://github.com/css-modules/css-modules) scopes "class names and animation names", so a type selector stays global. The prefix did not keep the host's rule out. Our take, not a rule: with few teams and a lint, a prefix or CSS Modules is often enough.

{% annotation line=3 %}
Discipline, not a boundary.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=5 %}
The module file still leaked.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=6 %}
Isolation out is not isolation in.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% fillBlanks id="f2" estSeconds=60 template="// remote_b/src/Widget.tsx: give the widget its own shadow tree\nconst shadow = el.___method___({ mode: 'open' });\n\n// render the widget's elements into it\nreturn createPortal(<button>clicked {count}</button>, ___target___);" language="ts" concepts=["shadow-dom-isolation"] %}
{% blank id="method" accepted=["attachShadow"] misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
attachShadow attaches a shadow DOM tree to the element and returns its ShadowRoot. Source: MDN, Element.attachShadow().
{% /blank %}
{% blank id="target" accepted=["shadow"] misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
A portal renders its children into the DOM node you pass. To land inside the shadow tree, pass the ShadowRoot itself. Source: react.dev, createPortal.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p4" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/shadow-with-link.ts" language="ts" concepts=["shadow-dom-isolation", "css-global-scope"] %}
{% option output="remote_b" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
That is what most teams expect after the move. But the CSS file still went into document.head, outside the shadow tree.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Pay (host), remote_a, remote_b" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
The page's stylesheet did not reach into the shadow tree. remote_b's own button was the only one that stayed grey.
{% /option %}
{% option output="Pay (host), remote_a" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. Upside down: the rule still hit the host and remote_a, and missed the only button it was written for.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So shadow DOM hid the button from its own team's CSS?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% pitfall id="pf1" estSeconds=40 concepts=["shadow-dom-isolation", "style-isolation-options"] %}
Team B moved the widget into a shadow root and shipped. Pay stayed red, and remote_b's own buttons went grey. [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components/Using_shadow_DOM) says page CSS "does not affect nodes inside the shadow DOM", and [global styles](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Scoping) don't apply to shadow trees. remote_b's stylesheet was still a page stylesheet. A shadow root only isolates the CSS that lives inside it.
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Two bugs for the price of one deploy. Thank you, Team B.
{% /bubble %}
{% gotcha %}
Shadow DOM moves the boundary. The remote's styles must move inside it too, or they style the page instead.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /pitfall %}

{% fillBlanks id="f3" estSeconds=60 template="// remote_b: ship the CSS inside the shadow tree, not in document.head\nimport css from './widget.css?___query___';\n\ncreatePortal(<><style>{css}</style><button>clicked {count}</button></>, shadow);" language="ts" concepts=["shadow-dom-isolation", "style-isolation-options"] %}
{% blank id="query" accepted=["inline"] misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
With ?inline, Rsbuild gives you the compiled CSS as a string inside the JavaScript bundle and generates no separate CSS file. The style element then puts it inside the shadow root. Source: Rsbuild, CSS usage.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p5" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/shadow-inherit.ts" language="ts" concepts=["shadow-dom-isolation"] %}
{% option output="heading black, button border 2px black" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
The border stayed out, but color is inherited. The shadow tree's top elements inherit from their host, and the host sits inside main.
{% /option %}
{% option output="heading blue, button border 3px green" misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
The host's button selector cannot match elements in another shadow tree, so the green border stayed outside. Only inherited values came in.
{% /option %}
{% option output="heading blue, button border 2px black" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. The button rule stopped at the boundary, but the inherited color flowed in. Sources: MDN, Inheritance; W3C CSS Scoping.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="says" %}
Oh. Fonts and colors from the host still come through. That's handy for themes, too.
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% recap id="rc1" estSeconds=30 concepts=["css-global-scope", "style-isolation-options", "shadow-dom-isolation"] %}
{% point %}
A remote's stylesheet is ==the page's stylesheet==. A type selector in it matches every element in the document.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
A prefix kept remote_b's rule in, not the host's rule out. CSS Modules rename classes; type selectors stay global.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
Shadow DOM only isolates CSS placed inside the shadow root. Inherited properties like color still cross from the host.
{% /point %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
One rule, three teams, zero code review. My kind of stylesheet.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /recap %}

{% predict id="p6" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/css-requests.ts" language="ts" concepts=["style-isolation-options", "shadow-dom-isolation"] %}
{% option output="run A: 1, run B: 1" misconception="isolation-is-free" %}
With ?inline there is no separate CSS file to fetch. The rule travels inside remote_b's JavaScript, so it arrives only with the code.
{% /option %}
{% option output="run A: 1, run B: 0" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. Run B fetched no CSS file. That is a cost too: the CSS now ships inside remote_b's JavaScript chunk.
{% /option %}
{% option output="run A: 0, run B: 0" misconception="css-stays-in-its-remote" %}
Run A did fetch a CSS file from :3002. It is the one that ended up in document.head and restyled the whole page.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% cliffhanger id="c1" estSeconds=20 nextLessonId="deploy-01" question="Team B fixes the leak and deploys remote_b at 11:20. A customer opened checkout at 11:15 and keeps the tab open. Which remote_b do they get, and what if the new one fails to load?" %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Next time I take down checkout with one deploy. No CSS needed.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /cliffhanger %}
